/* Which LCDC bits are RELEASED from the two-stage conflict -- a released bit
 * takes its new value at the intermediate dot instead of holding the old one.
 * 0 = nothing released, which is the shipped `old | (v & BG_EN)` expression
 * exactly, so the default is a bit-identical control by construction.
 *
 * The suite's own `2` variants say what this family probes: "sprites are
 * positioned to cause the write to occur on different T-cycles of the
 * background tile fetch, showing when the change to THE BIT takes effect" --
 * per bit, not per register. We apply one shape to all eight. */
/* WLEAD_BITS / WLEAD_DOTS -- which LCDC bits settle ahead of the rest of the
 * register at a mid-mode-3 write, and by how many dots.  A MASK, like LCDC_REL,
 * because the suite's own `2` variants say this family is per bit, not per
 * register.  0x00 / 0 = shipped.  See the LCDC write handler. */
/* OBJ_POS_HOLD -- the output may not advance past a position at which an object
 * is still owed, judged by position alone.  0 = shipped.  See the window-restart
 * arm in ppu_tick_1. */
/* SHIPPED AT 1 on the maintainer's ruling.  Registered in the PPU design §25.
 * BASE 1718 -> 1630, re-derived at that base when shipped: m3_lcdc_win_map_change
 * 40 -> 0 and m3_wx_4_change_sprites 8 -> 0, two EXACT ZEROS, with
 * m3_lcdc_tile_sel_win_change 454 -> 414.  Exactly three cartridges differ in raw
 * bytes and they are precisely the three the counter fires on.
 *
 * THE RECLAIMED PASS.  m3_wx_4_change_sprites was carried RED BY NAME as the
 * object fork's only LOST PASS -- "a pass is not just pixels and one px is the
 * whole of it".  It returns to PASS here.  Recorded as a reclaim under the
 * register's reclaim protocol rather than absorbed into the pixel total.
 *
 * THE FIRING COUNTER IS THE CHECK, AND IT IS THE STANDARD RATHER THAN A
 * NICETY OF THIS PACKET.  It was run BEFORE the score, and it separates a
 * placement fault from a mechanism fault: a build that scores wrongly and fires
 * zero is misplaced; one that fires correctly and scores wrongly is wrong.
 * Required, and measured exact on all six including all three zeros:
 *
 *     m3_lcdc_win_map_change        80        m3_lcdc_obj_size_change        0
 *     m3_lcdc_tile_sel_win_change   80        m3_lcdc_obj_en_change          0
 *     m3_wx_4_change_sprites       160        win_en_change_multiple_wx      0
 *
 * ANYONE CHANGING THIS CODE RUNS THAT COUNTER FIRST.  Build with -DPOSCEN and
 * count POSHOLD lines per cartridge; a matching score with a wrong count is not
 * this mechanism.
 *
 * NOTE 2026-10-17 (gb-statdocs r4/dmg NOTES 14): the 80 / 80 / 160 above
 * are WHOLE-RUN counts from the section-22 era.  Measured per DISPLAYED FRAME on
 * the core of 15 October they are 8 / 8 / 0 (m3_wx_4_change_sprites no longer
 * fires on its scored frame after later changes); the GB tab prints the
 * per-frame census.  The figures above are kept as the record of that ship.
 *
 * Mode 3 must not move: the clause claims no movement, so any movement refutes
 * the shape outright.  It does not move -- and the criterion is LIVE but ONE
 * PROBE WIDE (§33.2): the hold fires on 1 of the probe corpus, and a positive
 * control that withholds the dot without crediting it back moves exactly that
 * probe by exactly +1 dot.  Real, and thin; not 214 probes' worth of evidence.
 *
 * Tier 2: none of the three cartridges has a DMG photograph. */
/* OBJ_PER_ACCESS -- the object-fetch restructure, stage one: INERTNESS ONLY.
 *
 * Today the object fetch is a CLOSED FORM.  A counter runs to a constant and at
 * the terminal dot ppu_fetch_object() does everything at once -- forms the
 * address, performs BOTH VRAM accesses, and merges.  Both oracles do not compute
 * the cost at all; it ELAPSES from the accesses.
 *
 * This rebuilds the fetch AS ITS ACCESSES, so each one is individually
 * schedulable.  At this setting BOTH accesses are still performed on the
 * terminal dot, so the rebuild MUST be bit-identical to the closed form -- that
 * is the whole claim of stage one, and nothing is allowed to change until it
 * holds.  Moving an access is a later act with its own registration.
 *
 * The two-address foundation is what makes this expressible: the fetch has
 * exactly two VRAM accesses, at addr and addr+1, and they were only ever
 * simultaneous because the closed form had nowhere else to put them.
 *
 *   0 = shipped closed form
 *   1 = per-access rebuild, accesses co-scheduled (inert) */
#ifndef OBJ_PER_ACCESS
#define OBJ_PER_ACCESS 0
#endif
/* OBJ_ACC_SPLIT -- the restructure's CORRECTION stage.  Requires OBJ_PER_ACCESS.
 *
 * Stage one put both accesses on the terminal dot, where the closed form had
 * them.  The mechanism says the accesses OCCUPY THE BUS FOR FIXED DURATIONS and
 * the two data addresses are FORMED SEPARATELY -- so the low byte's access
 * occupies its own period before the high byte's, and each forms its address at
 * its own dot.  Where something changes the address between them, the two halves
 * of one row come from different places.  That is the two-address defect.
 *
 * NO FREE PARAMETER, and none is available: the split is at the access boundary,
 * one access period before the terminal dot.  A clause needing a stage count or
 * a fraction of an accumulated count would be out of scope by the registration's
 * structural disqualifier, and this needs neither.
 *
 *   0 = accesses co-scheduled (stage one, inert)
 *   1 = low byte one access period earlier, address formed at its own dot */
#ifndef OBJ_ACC_SPLIT
#define OBJ_ACC_SPLIT 0
#endif
/* OBJ_HI_W -- the LCDC settling width the HIGH byte's address is formed at.
 * 0 keeps the shipped single address. */
#ifndef OBJ_HI_W
#define OBJ_HI_W 13
#endif
