/* TSEL_SET_LASTU -- THE SETTING GLITCH SUBSTITUTES THE LAST BYTE READ IN THE
 * $8000 (UNSIGNED) ADDRESSING MODE, WHOEVER READ IT.  SOURCE-READ (docboy,
 * its CGB tile-data-select change handling: the substitute is the last tile
 * data ever read in unsigned addressing mode, object fetches included).
 *
 * RESTATED: the stash is written by every object data read (objects are always
 * $8000-based) AND by every background/window data read whose address was
 * formed on the $8000 base.  A substituted byte writes nothing.  The stash
 * persists across lines.  The author's ranked list ("bitplane 1 of the most
 * recently drawn sprite, if any, or ...the most recently drawn tile...") is
 * the special case where the last such read was an object's.
 *
 * ONE ORDERING RULE OF OUR OWN, AND ITS REASON: our fetcher lets an object
 * fetch land between a tile's low and high data steps.  On hardware the
 * object fetch waits for the background fetcher to finish its tile (Pan Docs:
 * the 0..5-dot wait in the object penalty), so the tile's high read PRECEDES
 * the object's reads.  A high read whose tile was interrupted by an object
 * therefore does not overwrite the object's bytes in the stash.
 *
 * WHAT IT CLOSES: m3_lcdc_tile_sel_change (CGB) line 128, 8 px.  Line 127's
 * $8000-based low read leaves $FF; the line-128 set edge takes it.  Line
 * 128's object (row 0) then leaves its own byte for line 129.
 * THE REASON FOR THE MEASUREMENT IS NOT ESTABLISHED: why a select change
 * mid-step yields a stale latch content rather than a fresh read is
 * docboy's statement, unexplained there.  0 = the previous rule (sprite only). */
