/* LAYER_PROBE -- a DIAGNOSTIC, never a candidate, and it changes no timing.
 * It rewrites only the ADDRESS a background/window tile fetch computes, on the
 * same dots, so a render taken under it differs from the shipped one exactly
 * where the displayed pixels actually depend on which source was read.
 *   1 = read the OTHER map (0x9800 <-> 0x9C00)
 *   2 = compute the address as if the window were NOT active
 * Lines that come out IDENTICAL are lines where the reference image cannot say
 * which map, or which layer, produced them -- which bounds what any pixel-level
 * characterisation of that cell is entitled to conclude. */
/* MERGE_EN_LAG -- Lab A's clause for m3_lcdc_obj_en_change.  The mixing stage
 * consults the object enable LIVE; hardware consults it as it stood ONE DOT
 * EARLIER.  During output a pixel and a dot are the same thing, which is why the
 * residual is a boundary displaced by exactly one pixel.
 *
 * NOT expressible in the per-bit width family: width 9 holds the old value only
 * while lcdc_late is set, the two dots around a write, and measured at base 1892
 * widths 7, 8 and 9 all give board 1138 -- re-derived at base 1138, having read
 * 1374 and 1892 at the two previous bases.  (The cell now reads 2 rather than
 * 56 because MERGE_EN_LAG shipped; the WIDTHS remain unobservable, which is the
 * claim.)  The clause is a
 * PERMANENT one-dot shadow at one consumer, not a conflict-window special case.
 *
 * FENCED TO THE ENABLE.  Bit 1 only, at OBJEN_MERGE only.  The object-palette
 * cell shows the same one-pixel displacement and is NOT covered here: Lab A
 * established the two are different mechanisms -- all three implementations read
 * the palette at the same point, so a read cannot be the difference there, while
 * for the enable the reads genuinely differ.  Reads identical, writes not; a
 * consultation defect against a delivery defect, with a coincident magnitude. */
/* SHIPPED AT 1 on the maintainer's ruling -- fourth packet.  Registered in
 * the PPU design 40 (pre-registration), 41 (measurement) and 45 (packet).
 * BASE 1374 -> 1266, re-derived at that base on shipping rather than quoted.
 *
 * His reason: re-derived at base, the prediction now measured rather than
 * assumed, tripwire held.
 *
 * WHAT IT IS, AND THE COST IS PART OF IT.  Not "54 of 56 fixed": ALL FIFTY-SIX
 * original pixels are gone and TWO ARE CREATED, at x=0 on lines 17 and 22, on
 * lines that were previously correct.  Fifty-six fixed, two created.
 *
 * THE SEPARATION CRITERION WAS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE AND IT PASSED AT BYTE LEVEL:
 * m3_obp0_change's frame is unchanged BIT FOR BIT, so the fence between this
 * clause and the palette displacement is a property of the implementation and
 * not of the intent.  A matching count would have left it a matter of intent.
 *
 * THE TRIPWIRE passed on what was registered -- the LINE SET and the runs of
 * m3_lcdc_bg_en_change, not its count.
 *
 * SCOPE: exactly two cartridges of 31 differ in raw bytes, both object-enable
 * cells.  The variant moves by the same 54 px and was NOT registered in advance;
 * it is recorded as an unregistered movement rather than folded into the result.
 *
 * MODE 3 IS STRUCTURALLY VACUOUS HERE AND IS NOT COUNTED.  OBJEN_MERGE has one
 * live consumer, the objwon shade decision, which touches no dot counter, no
 * fetch step, no lx and nothing gating whether an object is fetched.  The merge
 * is downstream of the fetcher, so no perturbation of it could ever move mode-3
 * length.  The table being identical carries no information.
 *
 * THE TWO CREATED PIXELS have a registered prediction of their own (section 43):
 * if the single-position exception is real, importing it fixes exactly those
 * four pixels -- two here and two on the variant, all at x=0 on lines 17 and 22
 * -- and nothing else.  Fixing them and moving anything else refutes it; fixing
 * neither refutes it.  It is NOT imported here, because a clause growing in
 * flight to cover the residual it produced is what the fence exists to prevent.
 *
 * Tier 2: neither cartridge has a DMG photograph. */
#ifndef MERGE_EN_LAG
#define MERGE_EN_LAG 1
#endif
/* ===========================================================================
 * LX0_LIVE -- ***THE FIRST VISIBLE PIXEL'S MERGE CONSULT.***  This is the
 * import the MERGE_EN_LAG block immediately above registered and declined to
 * make in flight.  Landed 2026-09-05 from our notes.
 *
 * ***ORACLE-DERIVED.***  Not MECHANISM.  Derived from docboy's construction,
 * read at docboy revision
 * bb4c525a68f099d31d1da0f0c7eac83f3efda5be, MIT, (c) 2025 Docheinstein.
 * docboy is byte-identical to expected/DMG-blob on all 24 DMG cells, which is
 * why it is the right instrument and exactly why it is NOT a second witness:
 * two emulators agreeing about a render is not evidence about silicon.
 * TIER 2; no photos/ read.  The label does not improve by being landed.
 *
 * RESTATED: the pixel-mixing stage of a DMG consults the LCD control register
 * as it stood one dot earlier -- except on the very first visible pixel of the
 * line, where it consults the register as it stands at that dot.  The
 * exception is ONE POSITION WIDE and covers BOTH bits the mixer needs:
 * background-window enable and object enable.
 *
 * SEPARATION: the source states the exception and names two test ROMs.  It
 * does NOT say what the exception is worth, where it fires, or that the two
 * bits fail differently on our side.  Reading the source established: that
 * the affected region is x = 0 and not tile (2,0); that the exception is only
 * observable when a CPU write to LCDC lands on the SAME DOT as the first
 * pixel's pop; and that our two bits fail for two different reasons -- one a
 * consultation fault, one a pipeline-stage mismatch.  That last CONTRADICTS
 * the natural reading of the source, which presents one exception over one
 * register.
 *
 * ***IT IS TWO CLAUSES AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME SHAPE.  DO NOT FUSE THEM.***
 *
 *   CLAUSE A, ppu_out_emit, LCDC.1.  At lx == 0 take the register LIVE at that
 *     dot, bypassing MERGE_EN_LAG's permanent one-dot shadow.  Everywhere else
 *     the shadow stands unchanged.  Worth 4 px on its own; m3_lcdc_obj_en_change
 *     and _variant both PASS.
 *
 *   CLAUSE B, LCDC.0.  ***A PIPELINE-STAGE CHANGE, NOT A VALUE CHANGE.***  We
 *     resolve the background blank when the pixel ENTERS the output queue
 *     (ppu_push_pixel); the reference resolves it when the pixel LEAVES it
 *     (ppu_out_emit).  So at lx == 0 we do NOT blank at insert, and blank at
 *     the POP instead -- through the SAME settled-width accessor,
 *     lcdc_cw(gb, REL_MERGE, LCDC_W_MERGE0), simply evaluated a stage later.
 *     Worth 4 px on its own; m3_lcdc_bg_en_change PASSES.
 *
 * ***THE OBVIOUS VERSION OF CLAUSE B IS WRONG AND WAS MEASURED WRONG.***
 * Taking LCDC.0 "live" at the INSERT site -- the same shape as clause A --
 * CREATED FOUR PIXELS (lines 11-14, x = 0) AND FIXED NONE: the cell went
 * 4 -> 8.  It is the STAGE that has to move, not the freshness of the value.
 * The write lands on the very dot the first pixel pops, which is why live and
 * lagged differ exactly there and nowhere else.
 *
 * DEVICE GATING.  The CGB reference AGREES WITH OUR WRONG VALUE at this
 * position, so an ungated clause would buy DMG with CGB.  The oracle applies
 * its own exception on DMG and NOT on CGB, and its author records having
 * verified that the position is not a special case there.  Both
 * clauses below are therefore carried on the DMG arm of GB_G17 -- the pixel
 * merge's own already-discharged row, which both sites already name -- so no
 * fence accounting moves and the CGB arm is textually untouched.
 *
 * WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM: clause B is SITED, not derived.  Deferring the
 * blank to the pop is what the reference does and what closes the cell.
 * NOTHING HERE SAYS THE POP IS WHERE SILICON DECIDES IT.  If the hardware
 * question is ever put, this is the value to re-decide.
 *
 * MEASURED at this landing, base 3857a7a, src/gb.c 30dc1f98db59 working-CRLF /
 * 57dd3c3a9e40 stored-LF, scored by our private board against
 * expected/DMG-blob:
 *
 *     LX0_LIVE 0 (control)   337 px, 16/24, ALL 24 RENDERS BYTE-IDENTICAL
 *     LX0_LIVE 1             329 px, 19/24
 *         m3_lcdc_bg_en_change           4 -> 0   PASSES
 *         m3_lcdc_obj_en_change          2 -> 0   PASSES
 *         m3_lcdc_obj_en_change_variant  2 -> 0   PASSES
 *         the other 21 cells                      BYTE-IDENTICAL
 *
 * EIGHT FIXED, ZERO CREATED, and the CGB board is unmoved at 2221 px 16/27. */
#ifndef LX0_LIVE
#define LX0_LIVE 1
#endif
/* SCX_HI_PROBE -- a DIAGNOSTIC, never a candidate, and it changes NO TIMING.
 * It offsets the background tile COLUMN by whole tiles, which is the high five
 * bits of SCX -- the quantity m3_scx_high_5_bits is about.  The fine scroll
 * (scx & 7), which is the part that costs dots, is untouched, so mode-3 length
 * cannot move and only the address arithmetic changes.
 *
 * A line identical under it is a line whose pixels do not depend on WHICH tile
 * column was fetched -- so the reference cannot say which coarse scroll produced
 * it.  That is the ambiguity that bounds any claim about this cell, and it is
 * not the one LAYER_PROBE measures. */
/* LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG -- Lab A's clause for the three derived line-0
 * displacements (the PPU design 57, registered in 58).
 *
 * Our mode-2 source rises ONE DOT LATER on line 0 -- dot 3 against dot 2 --
 * which is correct and matches the oracle.  But our dispatch is pinned at dot 5
 * on both, so the difference is ABSORBED: a raise at 2 and a raise at 3 precede
 * the same sample and dispatch identically.
 *
 * In hardware the CPU samples once per instruction.  A sample lying between
 * those two dots catches the ordinary-line raise and MISSES the line-0 one,
 * deferring dispatch by a whole machine cycle -- the four dots those three
 * cartridges compensate for.  We catch both, so their compensation
 * over-corrects against us by exactly four.
 *
 * THE RAISE IS NOT TOUCHED, and that is deliberate: the raise is already right,
 * and a clause that moved it would be treating a correct thing as the defect.
 * This holds the STAT bit out of the DISPATCH ELIGIBILITY TEST for a few dots on
 * line 0, which is the sample the hardware misses.  Value is in dots; 4 is one
 * machine cycle.  0 = shipped.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IF YOU ARE HERE BECAUSE THIS KNOB LOOKS LIKE A LARGE FREE WIN: at 4 it takes
 * the board 1374 -> 1246 and 6/24 -> 11/24, five new passes.  IT IS NOT SHIPPED
 * AND THE REASON IS NOT CAUTION.  Read both halves below, in this order.
 *
 * THE MECHANISM IS CONFIRMED.  Measured: dispatch moves 5 -> 9 on line 0 and
 * stays at 5 on all 143 ordinary lines -- exactly one machine cycle, line 0
 * only.  The raise is untouched, because the raise is already correct.  Mode 3
 * is unmoved across all 252 probes with the SCX ramp exact, and that criterion
 * is REAL here rather than vacuous: a dispatch change has no route to length,
 * so movement would have refuted the clause outright.
 *
 * THE ACCOUNT'S SCOPE IS REFUTED.  The account predicted THREE cells -- the
 * three whose 4-dot compensation was derived forcedly from their own handlers --
 * and fenced everything else.  Measured, NINE OF THE ELEVEN line-0 residuals on
 * this board went to zero, including cells whose magnitudes do not follow from a
 * fixed 4-dot compensation, and including THE FENCED CELL: m3_scy_change 58 -> 4,
 * its entire 54-px line-0 residual gone.  Reaching that cell was registered in
 * advance as meaning the account is wrong about what it explains rather than
 * that it did better than expected.
 *
 * SO WHAT IS UNESTABLISHED IS THE SCOPE, not the mechanism.  Nobody knows which
 * pixels this clause OWNS as against merely MOVES, and the two are not
 * distinguished by any evidence we currently hold.  A packet cannot be assembled
 * until that is settled.
 *
 * AND ONE CONSEQUENCE THAT IS THE MAINTAINER'S ALONE: m3_scy_change's 58 px are cycle 1's
 * surviving check, and the note at OBJ_FETCH_FINISH says driving them to
 * zero WITHOUT a mechanism that owns line 0 destroys the evidence for that ship.
 * This clause claims to be such a mechanism.  Either it owns those pixels and
 * the check has been retired by the mechanism it was waiting for, or it
 * merely moves them and this is exactly what the fence exists to catch.
 * NOTHING IN THE CURRENT EVIDENCE DISTINGUISHES THOSE.  See the PPU design
 * sections 57 to 59. */
/* SHIPPED AT 4 on the maintainer's ruling -- fifth packet.  Registered in
 * the PPU design 58, measured in 59, packet in 60.  His reason: the fence's
 * condition is met on the REGISTERED evidence -- 54 of 54, both directions, on a
 * threshold set before derivation -- and a packet carrying its own refuted first
 * scope claim is the form rulings should arrive in.
 *
 * READ 60.1 BEFORE TRUSTING THIS.  The mechanism was confirmed first; the account
 * attached to it was then REFUTED ON SCOPE, predicting three cells where nine
 * moved; and the scope was re-established afterwards BY A DIFFERENT ROUTE,
 * deriving the alignment per boundary rather than testing residual form.  The
 * first scope claim being wrong is why the second is credible. */
/* 2026-10-28: 3, not 4.  The running look (RUN_IRQ_LOOK_EDGES 3)
 * samples two dots ahead, so a 4-dot hold hid the line-0 raise from the sample
 * that must see it and line 0 dispatched one machine cycle late against every
 * other line (mealybug: line 0 alone wrong in 12 cells).  At 3 the raise is
 * visible at dot 7, inside the look from the dot-5 boundary; a halted or
 * look-free sample (boundaries at 5 and 9) cannot tell 3 from 4. */
#ifndef LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG
#define LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG 3
#endif
/* LYC_CARRY and LYC_DISPATCH_LAG -- the two clauses that close
 * daid/ppu_scanline_bgp.gb (DMG).  Read both; the second is meaningless
 * without the first.
 *
 * WHAT THE CARTRIDGE MEASURES.  It writes BGP ten times per scanline, four
 * M-cycles apart, in a handler exactly 114 M-cycles long -- one scanline --
 * so the rendered image IS a picture of when a BGP write takes effect.  The
 * frame's whole phase is set ONCE, by the LYC=0 STAT dispatch, and carried by
 * the handler's own self-timing thereafter.
 *
 * THE FAULT, MEASURED IN THE CORE AND NOT INFERRED FROM THE RESIDUAL.  At the
 * base of this change the LYC=0 comparison is true continuously from line 153
 * dot 12 through line 0, yet it raises TWICE: once at line 153 dot 12
 * (correct), and again at line 0 dot 3, because the comparator's
 * start-of-line blind spot drops the flag and it rises again.  The second
 * dispatch RE-ENTERS the handler and restarts its data pointer, which puts the
 * whole schedule ONE SCANLINE late; the line-0 dispatch phase then puts it
 * EIGHT DOTS early within the line.  Both displacements are removed here, and
 * neither of them is a colour-table effect.
 *
 * LYC_CARRY -- MECHANISM.  A level comparator cannot produce a rising edge
 * when the comparison never changed.  The blind spot governs what the
 * comparator READS; it may not manufacture a falling edge on a match that is
 * already true.  Measured blast radius: exactly one event per frame, and only
 * where LY=LYC carries across a line boundary -- which is LYC=0 at the 153->0
 * wrap and nowhere else, because ly_for_cmp differs across every other
 * boundary.  TCAGBD 8.9.1's line-153 table is untouched: there the compared
 * value CHANGES (153 then 0), so no carry applies and both LYC=153 and LYC=0
 * still fire one machine cycle apart.
 *
 * LYC_DISPATCH_LAG -- FITTED-TO-REFERENCE, under the maintainer's bounded exception for
 * this row (2026-09-17), which permits OUR fit at a site where it costs
 * nothing.  It is the SAME CLAUSE as LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG above, in the same
 * units, at the same stage -- the dispatch eligibility sample, never the raise
 * -- applied to the LYC source instead of to line 0.  The derivation of the
 * PLACEMENT is ours: with LYC_CARRY alone the picture is four dots early, the
 * winning interval is 2..5 dots, that interval is exactly one machine cycle
 * wide, and 4 is the machine cycle.  A dispatch sample can only move in whole
 * machine cycles, so the constant is the quantum and not a tuned number.
 *
 * WHY IT IS RESTRICTED TO THE LYC SOURCE, MEASURED AND NOT ASSUMED.  The
 * all-source form of the same lag costs 20 of the 24 mealybug DMG cells and
 * takes the CGB mealybug board 8 -> 27503 px.  Every mealybug cartridge is
 * driven by a MODE-2 sourced STAT interrupt out of a nop slide; this cartridge
 * by an LYC-sourced one out of HALT.  That restriction is the whole of why
 * this placement is free.
 *
 * PRICE, MEASURED ON A CLEAN CLONE OF 80da9df (tree GameBoy11):
 *   board            253 -> 254 PASS, ONE row changed   our sb4run tool, both boots
 *   mealybug DMG     24/24 -> 24/24, 0 px               our run_mealybug tool
 *   mealybug CGB     8 px / 26 of 27, unmoved           our cgbboard tool
 *   dmg-acid2 DMG    PIXEL-PERFECT, unmoved             our net tool
 *   dmg-acid2 CGB    0 px, unmoved                      our acid2cgb tool
 *   mode-3 table     identical, all 252 probes          our mode3_probe tool --check
 *   our net tool --quick   identical to control but the wall-clock line
 *   Mooneye          95 PASS / 2 known deviations, both unmoved
 *
 * AND IT IS NOT A TOLERANCE PASS.  Our DMG render is BYTE-IDENTICAL to
 * ppu_scanline_bgp_1.dmg.png -- 0 of 23040 pixels differ AT ALL -- and it
 * stands 464 px from each of the other two references, which is exactly the
 * distance those two stand from reference 1.  The three references are the
 * three instance-dependent per-pixel outcomes the suite's own our daid tool
 * describes; reference 1 is the OR (black-line) outcome, which is the one our
 * shipped BGP_OR = 1 predicts.  Which reference we land on was not chosen.
 *
 * THE CGB HALF IS NOT CLOSED AND MUST NOT BE READ AS CLOSED.  These two
 * clauses take it 6734 -> 1728 px and it still FAILS.  Its remainder is ONE
 * PIXEL at each of eight BGP boundaries, and the only constant that reaches it
 * is BGP_LEAD_CGB 3 -> 4, which WAS measured and costs FIVE protected CGB
 * cells: m3_bgp_change 0 -> 864, m3_bgp_change_sprites 0 -> 716,
 * m3_lcdc_obj_en_change_variant 0 -> 144, m3_window_timing 0 -> 138,
 * m3_window_timing_wx_0 0 -> 144.  That is the fence, and the CGB row stays
 * declined on it.  LYC_DISPATCH_LAG_CGB exists so the CGB arm is separable;
 * at 8 it takes the CGB row 6734 -> 576 px at no measured cost anywhere, but
 * it still does not win the row, so it is left inheriting the DMG value. */
#ifndef LYC_CARRY
#define LYC_CARRY 1
#endif
/* LYC_BLANK_HOLD (8 Nov 2026) -- MECHANISM, original Game Boy only.
 * At the start of a line the comparator is blind for one machine cycle and LY
 * already holds the new line.  That blind spot is what STAT bit 2 READS (0);
 * it is not a falling edge on the interrupt line.  A match from the line
 * before (LY=LYC on line N) keeps the line high until the blind spot ends, and
 * by then the new line's mode-2 source is already high, so line N+1 raises no
 * mode-2 STAT interrupt: "STAT blocking" across the boundary.  Without it a
 * game that runs LYC=N with the mode-2 select on takes a spurious interrupt at
 * every N to N+1 boundary.  Pinball Deluxe does exactly that (LYC=126): its
 * per-line handler then runs one line late, misses line 144's mode 3, spins
 * through VBlank and nests the VBlank handler once a frame until the stack
 * runs over the game's variables (the broken table and the lock-up).  The same
 * row was found and fixed once before as D8 (see the mode-2 source note in
 * ppu_update_stat_line); the blind-spot form that LYC_CARRY left in place
 * reopened it.  Hardware tests (gambatte, DMG): 7 rows move to pass
 * (lycm2int_m2irq_1, lyc0m2int_m2irq_1, lyc143_late_m2enable_lycdisable_2,
 * enable_after_lycint_1, late_enable_after_lycint_1 and _2,
 * lyc1_m2irq_late_lyc255_3) and 3 move to fail (lycm2int_m2irq_2,
 * lyc0m2int_m2irq_2, enable_after_lycint_2): each of the 3 is the second half
 * of a pair whose first half now passes, read one machine cycle later, so they
 * passed before only because of the spurious interrupt and now show a
 * one-cycle offset that was hidden behind it.  Net +4, TRADE taken;
 * SameBoy 1.0.5 holds the line the same way (lyc_interrupt_line is not cleared
 * while ly_for_comparison is -1, models up to CGB-C at single speed).  The
 * Color keeps its own comparator arm (CT_*), unmeasured here.  Off arm: 0. */
#ifndef LYC_BLANK_HOLD
#define LYC_BLANK_HOLD 1
#endif
/* 2026-10-28: 1, not 4.  The same correction as LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG:
 * with the running look at the line start (RUN_IRQ_LOOK_EDGES 3) a 4-dot hold
 * hid the coincidence raise from the look's sample and daid/ppu_scanline_bgp
 * (DMG) failed (55 checkpoints moved).  Swept 3, 2, 1: only 1 gives that row
 * byte-identical to the served core; a halted sample cannot tell 1 from 4. */
#ifndef LYC_DISPATCH_LAG
#define LYC_DISPATCH_LAG 1
#endif
/* IRQ_HOLD_FRESH_ONLY -- THE LINE-0 / LYC HOLD DELAYS ONLY A NEW REQUEST
 * (2026-10-25).
 * LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG and LYC_DISPATCH_LAG hide a fresh STAT raise from the
 * processor for a few dots: the request bit is set, the processor's sample
 * misses it.  They were armed on every rising edge of the STAT line, even when
 * the STAT request bit was ALREADY SET (written into IF by the program, or
 * raised earlier and not yet taken).  A request the processor could already
 * see then vanished from irq_visible() for the hold, and a dispatch whose
 * second sample fell inside it found no request and took vector $0000 (the
 * "all bits cancelled" path).  Found on the Pokemon Gold/Silver Spaceworld 1997
 * demos on the Game Boy: the program writes IF = $02, then EI and CALL; the
 * hold armed between the boundary test and the re-sample, the dispatch went to
 * $0000, RST $38, crash.  LABEL: MECHANISM (a propagation delay belongs to the
 * edge that is propagating; a latch that is already set has no edge to delay).
 * Only the original Game Boy's holds are reached (irq_visible returns before
 * them on the Color).  Off arm: 0 restores the old arming. */
#ifndef IRQ_HOLD_FRESH_ONLY
#define IRQ_HOLD_FRESH_ONLY 1
#endif
/* ===========================================================================
 *  CT -- THE COLOR'S CPU-VISIBLE TIMING (2026-10-24).
 *
 *  WHAT IT IS.  On the Color, every time the CPU can SEE -- when LY changes,
 *  when STAT's mode bits change, when each STAT / vertical-blank request rises,
 *  when a halted CPU wakes, when a running CPU takes the interrupt, how long the
 *  speed switch takes -- is set here per speed, against the pinned reference's
 *  OUTPUTS (SameBoy 213a12ce, model CGB-C, public API; its source was read for
 *  the reasons, see below).  Nothing in this block moves a pixel by itself: the
 *  STAT mode bits the CPU reads get their own field (ct_stat); mode_report,
 *  which every VRAM / OAM access window keys off, keeps its master timing.
 *
 *  WHY.  Magical Drop (USA) rewrites one colour 46 times per line from an LYC
 *  interrupt and a STAT poll at double speed (22 Oct).  Its writes land
 *  in the palette's busy window only if the CPU reaches them on the same dot as
 *  on the hardware the reference models.  Fixing the palette window alone
 *  (CGB_PAL_BLOCK) moved it further from the reference because our STAT edges,
 *  requests and wake-ups were 1-4 dots off at double speed, in a pattern no
 *  single knob could fix (GB2's research arms broke Demotronic and Shantae).
 *
 *  HOW IT WAS MEASURED (instruments in test/cgb-ct, all restorable):
 *    edge.asm   48 cells: LY / STAT / IF read k machine cycles after a HALT wake
 *               on LYC 0/24/90/143/153, mode 2, mode 0, vertical blank, both speeds
 *    edgeb.asm  the same with the speed switched while the LCD is on (5 cells)
 *    disp.asm   dispatch from a RUNNING CPU (vector fetch time), both speeds
 *    our objgen tool  object lines: mode-3 length for 124 object layouts
 *    palblk     112 palette-window / STAT cells
 *    traces     access-by-access alignment of Magical Drop, Demotronic, Shantae
 *               against the reference (our salign tool), with the reference's
 *               display forced to sync every instruction (SYNC=1): unsynced,
 *               the reference reports a stale mode 3 on object lines it was not
 *               watching (non-monotonic STAT samples).
 *
 *  LABELS.  Every number below is FITTED (convergence, labelled): it is the
 *  reference's observed timing expressed in our dot ticks.  The following are
 *  MECHANISM, read in the reference's source with the reason stated:
 *    - a HALTED Color samples the request and then advances one machine cycle
 *      before it leaves HALT (the reference's CGB halt loop: sample, advance 4,
 *      wake on the sample) -- this is guard row G31's CGB arm (CT_HALT);
 *    - a RUNNING CPU samples the request as it stands at the instruction
 *      boundary, every source, no coincidence / line-0 holds (CT_RUNOLD 0);
 *    - at double speed the comparator's blind window HOLDS the old flag instead
 *      of clearing it (the reference only clears it on CGB <= C at single
 *      speed) -- G14's double-speed arm (CT_HOLD_DS);
 *    - the power-on line has no mode-0 source until its mode 3 ends;
 *    - the palette port opens 3 dots into mode 3 and closes 4 dots after the
 *      last pixel at double speed (the reference's blocked flag: set 2 dots
 *      after mode 3 starts, cleared 3 dots after the end) -- CT_PAL*_DS;
 *    - an H-blank transfer spends one byte slot before its first byte, and its
 *      request comes 3 dots after the last pixel at double speed.
 *  FITTED and load-bearing, said outright:
 *    - LCD_ON_LEN_CGB2 448 (was 451, "UNDECIDED -- UNREACHABLE"): our double-
 *      speed line runs 3 dots earlier against the LCD switch-on, which lines our
 *      mode 2 / mode 3 / last pixel up with the reference's exactly;
 *    - CT_SWITCH_PPU 2: a speed switch with the LCD on moves the PPU onto the
 *      other speed's alignment (single speed keeps the board's 450);
 *    - CT_SWITCH_SHORT 4: our speed-switch stall was 4 machine cycles longer.
 *  CHECK.  Any of the probe cells, the daid speed_switch_timing rows or the
 *  three title traces disagreeing with the reference at these values; a
 *  hardware capture of any edge in the probes.  Off arm: CT_ON 0 restores the
 *  master timing exactly (and LCD_ON_LEN_CGB2 451).
 *
 *  OPEN, measured and left: two objects 3 pixels apart (first at X = 65 or 67
 *  in the double-speed probe, 66 at single speed) end mode 3 one dot later than
 *  the reference -- our object comb (OBJ_READY_PHASE) does not follow the
 *  fetcher's phase after an object fetch.  Magical Drop meets it at frame 539
 *  line 132 (objects at 67 and 70).
 * ======================================================================== */
#ifndef CT_ON
#define CT_ON 1
#endif
#ifndef CT_SS
#define CT_SS 1                /* 0: the block applies at double speed only     */
#endif
#ifndef CT_SS_VB
#define CT_SS_VB 1             /* with CT_SS 0: keep the line-144 timing anyway  */
#endif
#ifndef CT_HALT
#define CT_HALT 1              /* MECHANISM, G31: sample, advance, then wake     */
#endif
