/* OBJ_FETCH_FINISH -- Lab A's mechanism for m3_scy_change and its four
 * relatives.  When an object becomes due, hardware does not stop the background
 * fetcher where it stands: the tile fetch in progress completes its map read and
 * both pixel-row reads at the dots they would have occurred on with NO OBJECT
 * PRESENT, and only then does the fetcher freeze for the object's data.  The
 * object's dots are paid afterwards; pixel output, not the fetcher, is what the
 * penalty holds back.
 *
 * Our core freezes mid-fetch.  Measured (our out/census test, FRZCEN build) at
 * base 2896 and RE-MEASURED AT BASE 1892 after this shipped and after the
 * position clause landed, IDENTICAL both times and identical again with the
 * position clause on -- which measures that clause's fetcher-timing neutrality
 * rather than expecting it: 1200 of 1440 freezes per test land BEFORE the
 * high-byte read, 880 of them with step 2 exactly pending.  The pending reads then resolve 5-10 dots later, on the
 * far side of the stall, against whatever the CPU wrote in the meantime.
 *
 * THE SHAPE, and why it is this shape rather than the obvious one.  Delaying the
 * START of the stall until the read completes grows mode 3 by 1-2 dots per object
 * and breaks intr_2_mode0_timing_sprites.  Instead the stall stays exactly where
 * it is and NOTHING about the fetcher's timing moves: what moves is only WHICH DOT
 * THE FETCH'S REGISTER INPUTS ARE SAMPLED ON.  During the stall's first dots we
 * snapshot SCY/SCX/LCDC at the dots each pending step would have executed on, and
 * the fetcher -- running at its normal, unchanged dots after the stall -- computes
 * its addresses from the snapshot instead of the live registers.  Mode 3's length
 * is therefore unchanged BY CONSTRUCTION, not by measurement.
 *
 * IF MODE 3 MOVES AT ALL, THE MODEL THIS RESTS ON IS WRONG and a pixel gain does
 * not rescue it.  See the PPU design, DESIGN NOTE - OBJ_FETCH_FINISH, 8.1.
 *
 * The snapshots are TAKEN unconditionally, so this code executes at the default
 * and elects the old behaviour; the knob gates only whether the fetcher consults
 * them.  That is the inertness proof: the new statements run, and the output does
 * not change.
 *
 *   0 = shipped: the fetch samples its registers wherever the stall leaves it
 *   1 = WHOLE-REGISTER form. SCY/SCX/LCDC are swapped wholesale for the step.
 *       Measured 2026-08-20: scy 558->58 (exact hit), tile_sel 512->8, but
 *       tile_sel_win 454->628 and bg_map/win_map flat. It over-reaches --
 *       swapping LCDC reaches lcdc_wincmp(), the window-enable comparison,
 *       which is a different consumer with its own timing -- and it under-
 *       reaches, because LCDC_W_BGMAP is 7 and resolves through lcdc_next,
 *       which a raw-register snapshot cannot see. Kept reachable so those
 *       figures stay reproducible.
 *   2 = PER-CONSUMER form. The snapshot captures each ADDRESS-ARITHMETIC
 *       consumer's OUTPUT through its own funnel accessor -- bg map select,
 *       win map select, tile-data select, SCX, SCY -- and nothing else.
 *       lcdc_wincmp() is deliberately NOT among them: window enable is not
 *       part of the address arithmetic and this mechanism does not describe
 *       it. Capturing the accessor's output rather than the register is also
 *       what reaches the width-7 lcdc_next path
 *
 * SHIPPED AT 2 -- the maintainer's ruling on the cycle 1 packet.  The FOLDED form: the
 * completion requirement with the window-restart discard inside it (see
 * fetcher_restart), exactly as measured, not the pre-fold pair.  Worth 1004 px
 * across two cartridges, board 2896 -> 1892, with every other cartridge of the
 * thirty-one BYTE-IDENTICAL to the core before it and mode 3 unmoved.
 *
 * THE SURVIVING CHECK, AND IT IS NOT A LEFTOVER.  m3_scy_change does not go
 * to zero.  It goes to 58.
 *
 * CORRECTED BY MEASUREMENT.  This said "those 58 px are all on LY 0", which I
 * wrote at the ship without measuring the row distribution.  Measured:
 * 54 px on LY 0, 3 px on LY 7, 1 px on LY 15.  Fifty-four of fifty-eight, not
 * fifty-eight of fifty-eight.  The check argument below is unaffected -- the
 * mechanism does not describe line 0 and could not have removed it -- but the
 * DESCRIPTION was wrong and is not left standing.  That was REGISTERED
 * BEFORE THE MEASUREMENT as the outcome that must survive: a result of 0 px
 * would have REFUTED this mechanism, because the mechanism does not describe
 * line 0 and could not have removed it.  The 58 surviving is part of the
 * evidence for what is shipped here.
 *
 * SO: ANYONE WHO DRIVES THOSE 58 PX TO ZERO WITHOUT A MECHANISM THAT OWNS LINE 0
 * HAS DESTROYED THE EVIDENCE FOR THIS SHIP, not improved the board.  The line-0
 * residual is a separate registered residual with its own account -- the
 * reference's entire line 0 is ours shifted by exactly one write rung across all
 * twenty column groups -- and it is closed by explaining that, never by tuning
 * this.  If you are here because the board says 58 and you want 0, read
 * the PPU design sections 6 and 9.3 first.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE TRIPWIRE ABOVE HAS BEEN ANSWERED -- READ THIS BEFORE CONCLUDING IT WAS
 * QUIETLY ABANDONED.  A mechanism that OWNS line 0 was found: the line-0
 * interrupt dispatch is one machine cycle late in this core (LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG,
 * the PPU design 57-60).  Its scope was established on this cell's own
 * content by a prediction registered before it was derived, passing 54 of 54 in
 * both directions.  So the condition the sentence above sets -- WITHOUT a
 * mechanism that owns line 0 -- is MET rather than breached.
 *
 * THAT CLAUSE IS NOW SHIPPED (the maintainer's ruling, LINE0_DISPATCH_LAG = 4), so
 * m3_scy_change READS 4 IN THIS TREE, not 58.  The remaining 4 px are on lines 7
 * and 15, which were never part of the check.  THE TRIPWIRE IS RETIRED --
 * it is not missing, not abandoned, and not tuned away.
 *
 * WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT DO.  The check was a TEST THAT WAS MADE AND
 * PASSED: 0 px would have refuted this mechanism, the result was 58, and that
 * result is historical and stays passed.  What is lost is the ONGOING TRIPWIRE,
 * not the original evidence.
 *
 * SO WEIGH THIS SHIP ON WHAT REMAINS, which is stated here so a later reader
 * does not have to reconstruct it: TWO HITS, one of them exactly the 500 px the
 * account placed OUTSIDE line 0 (m3_scy_change 558 -> 58) and the other
 * m3_lcdc_tile_sel_change 512 -> 8; EVERY OTHER CARTRIDGE BYTE-IDENTICAL, 29 of
 * 31, not merely equal in score; and MODE 3 UNCHANGED across 214 probes both
 * columns at that base.
 *
 * The tripwire is answered.  The evidence is not the tripwire.
 *
 * Tier: this is agreement between two emulators, not hardware.  m3_scy_change has
 * no DMG photograph, and neither does m3_lcdc_tile_sel_change.  Every figure in
 * this block is a score against the suite author's own render.  IT MUST NEVER BE
 * QUOTED AS HARDWARE. */
/* OBJ_FETCH_FINISH's definition WAS HERE, nested inside #ifndef OBJ_PHASE_WAIT.
 * Moved to top level below: while it sat here, -DOBJ_PHASE_WAIT=... removed the
 * definition of a SHIPPED mechanism entirely and it evaluated as 0 in #if --
 * silently reverting cycle 1 while looking like a normal measurement. */
#define OBJ_PHASE_WAIT 2
#endif
#ifndef OBJ_FETCH_FINISH
#define OBJ_FETCH_FINISH 2
#endif
/* SNAPLAG -- the DOT AT WHICH a frozen fetch step's register inputs are read,
 * relative to the dot that step is CREDITED to.  It changes no dot, no step
 * and no owed count: OBJ_FETCH_FINISH decides WHICH dot each frozen step is
 * credited to and SNAPLAG decides WHICH DOT'S VALUES are recorded there.
 *
 * 0 = shipped behaviour and emits NOTHING: every part of this knob is inside
 * `#if SNAPLAG != 0`, so at the default the preprocessed token stream is
 * IDENTICAL to before it existed.  1 records the values as they stood ONE DOT
 * EARLIER -- the inputs latched at the START of the step's execute dot rather
 * than at its end.
 *
 * ***FITTED, DELIBERATELY NOT MECHANISM.***  Nothing derives the one-dot lead.
 * The SHAPE is precedented -- WX_W_WINCMP, BGP_LEAD/OBP_LEAD and lcdc_cw all
 * carry per-consumer settling widths -- but A PRECEDENT IS NOT A DERIVATION,
 * and no hardware statement says the address inputs are read a dot before the
 * read they feed.  A score selects it.  What would earn MECHANISM: a document
 * or a device pinning the input-latch dot within a two-dot fetcher step.
 *
 * MEASURED at base 743e8727 (board 437), clean build per arm, blast radius by
 * BYTE COMPARISON of all 31 renders and never by an equal board figure:
 *   board 437 -> 434, 15/24 unchanged, m3_scy_change 4 -> 1
 *   ONE of 31 raws differs.  3 FIXED / 0 CREATED / 0 SHUFFLED, and the cell
 *   is 0.0% blind at this fault class so all three are DISCRIMINATING.
 *   m3_lcdc_tile_sel_change -- which PASSES at 0 px while the snapshot does
 *   648 effective substitutions on 72 of its lines -- is BYTE-IDENTICAL.
 *
 * MODE 3 CANNOT MOVE, BY CONSTRUCTION: the only writes below are to
 * fsnap_scy/fsnap_scx/fsnap_lcdc and the shadow.  No dot counter, no
 * fetch_step, no fetch_owed is touched.  Verifiable by reading this diff.
 *
 * THE (15,0) RESIDUAL IS CLOSED by the seed above.  It was NOT a phase
 * fault -- a two-dot lead leaves it and destroys the LY 7 fix -- it was
 * this knob failing to apply on odd `owed`. */
#ifndef SNAPLAG
#define SNAPLAG 1
#endif
/* SNAPLAG IS A BOOLEAN, NOT A MAGNITUDE, AND THE GUARD SAYS SO IN THE BUILD.
 * The shadow above is ONE DOT DEEP, so -DSNAPLAG=2 would be BYTE-IDENTICAL to
 * -DSNAPLAG=1 while reading like a two-dot lead -- a check aimed at an arm
 * that switches nothing (our method).  A two-dot lead needs a two-deep shadow
 * and is a DIFFERENT knob; it is not reachable by widening this one.
 */
#if SNAPLAG != 0 && SNAPLAG != 1
#error "SNAPLAG is 0 or 1. A wider lead needs a deeper shadow -- see the block above."
#endif
#ifndef OBJ_FIRST_RUN
#define OBJ_FIRST_RUN 0
#else
#define OBJ_FIRST_RUN_EXPLICIT 1
#endif
/* A DEAD KNOB MUST FAIL LOUDLY, NOT READ AS A FREE NULL.  Both of these live
 * only in the OBJ_PHASE_WAIT==0 arm -- OBJ_STALL_BIAS inside the #else of the
 * phase-keyed wait, OBJ_FIRST_RUN through obj_gap_armed, which that arm is the
 * only reader of.  At the SHIPPED OBJ_PHASE_WAIT=2 they are both inert, so
 * -DOBJ_STALL_BIAS=-4, =0 and =+8 all score identically and an arm sweeping
 * either one MEASURES NOTHING while reading as a clean null result.  Verified
 * behaviourally 2026-09-18: at the shipped configuration -DOBJ_FIRST_RUN=1,
 * -DOBJ_STALL_BIAS=4 and -DOBJ_STALL_BIAS=-4 every one gives the same 61 of 105
 * on intr_2_mode0_timing_sprites as the default.
 *
 * This is the same family as the warn-without-state defect already fixed
 * elsewhere in this tree: a check that prints an absence and sets no state.
 * Passing either knob on the command line at a configuration that cannot read
 * it is now a BUILD FAILURE that names the reachable configuration. */
#if (defined(OBJ_STALL_BIAS_EXPLICIT) || defined(OBJ_FIRST_RUN_EXPLICIT)) && OBJ_PHASE_WAIT
#error "OBJ_STALL_BIAS / OBJ_FIRST_RUN are DEAD at OBJ_PHASE_WAIT != 0 (shipped 2). Any sweep over either at the shipped configuration measures NOTHING. Build with -DOBJ_PHASE_WAIT=0 to reach them, or drop the -D."
#endif
#ifndef OBJ_AX_SCX
#define OBJ_AX_SCX 1
#endif
#ifndef OBJ_ROW_MASK
#define OBJ_ROW_MASK 1
#endif
/* ***THE SINGLE-POSITION EXCEPTION IS REFUTED. TESTED 2026-09-02, NOT UNTESTED,
 * AND NOT MERELY UNEXPLAINED.***  Read this before reopening the clause.
 *
 * The MERGE_EN_LAG block registers a prediction from the PPU design 43: that
 * importing the single-position exception would fix EXACTLY four pixels -- two on
 * m3_lcdc_obj_en_change and two on the variant, all at x=0 on lines 17 and 22 --
 * "and nothing else.  Fixing them and moving anything else refutes it; FIXING
 * NEITHER REFUTES IT."  It was deliberately left unimported at that ship.
 *
 * IT HAS NOW BEEN IMPORTED AND MEASURED.  OBJ_EN_EXC = 0 is exactly that import:
 * it gates the exception on (obj_fetching || lx == 0) instead of applying it
 * unconditionally.  At base 28ef39b2, clean build, byte comparison of all 31:
 *
 *     blast radius   2 renders, both obj_en cells, nothing else  <- SCOPE HELD
 *     obj_en_change    2 ->  46 px      variant   98 -> 142 px
 *     ACROSS BOTH      0 FIXED, 88 CREATED, 0 shuffled           <- DIRECTION FAILED
 *
 * IT FIXES NEITHER NAMED PIXEL.  (17,0) and (22,0) are untouched on both cells.
 * The registered outcome "fixing neither refutes it" therefore FIRES, on the
 * prediction's own stated terms.  Board would go 544 -> 632.
 *
 * POSITIVE CONTROL, so the negative is not a blind instrument: MERGE_EN_LAG = 0
 * moves the same two renders and takes the cell 2 -> 56 px, INDEPENDENTLY
 * REPRODUCING the historic 56 that the MERGE_EN_LAG block records.  That record
 * is CONFIRMED, not stale.
 *
 * SO THE FOUR PIXELS AT (17,0) AND (22,0) HAVE NO SURVIVING ACCOUNT -- their only
 * registered one is dead by measurement.  That is REFUTED, which is a STRONGER
 * status than unexplained and must not decay into it: anyone reopening this
 * clause is reopening something already killed, and owes a new mechanism first. */
#ifndef OBJ_EN_EXC
#define OBJ_EN_EXC 1
#endif
#ifndef STAT7
#define STAT7 0
#endif
